│ patch_name               │ trie_safe_reorder                                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ The original implementation could corrupt row contents and column identity during         │
│                          │ recursive reconstruction. In particular, it rebuilt frames from `.values.tolist()`, wrote │
│                          │ asynchronous results using completion-order indices, added and removed an                 │
│                          │ `original_index` column, merged frames back together, and finally returned an empty       │
│                          │ `column_orderings` list. This violates validation requirements because the returned data  │
│                          │ can no longer be reliably associated with the original cells, especially for mixed types, │
│                          │ duplicate values, and non-deterministic thread completion order.                          │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The stdout contains only diagnostic column prints and no Python exception, which          │
│                          │ indicates validation rejected the returned result rather than a crash. The replacement    │
│                          │ removes destructive recursion, temporary index columns, mutation, merging, and debug      │
│                          │ output. It preserves every original row and cell value, builds bounded deterministic      │
│                          │ column-order candidates using normalized serialization only for scoring, selects the best │
│                          │ candidate using sorted-string LCP Trie reuse, and returns valid per-row column orderings. │
